 "docs": {
  "en": {
   "title": "Western Canada snow cover from space: 201,390 km² (13 November)",
   "description": "201,390 km²: that is the snow-covered ground our model counts in Western Canada today. Last clear view of every place, up to 13 November: that is …",
   "lede": "201,390 km²: that is the snow-covered ground our model counts in Western Canada today. Last clear view of every place, up to 13 November: that is what our satellite layer keeps. Below, the snowiest resorts in Western Canada. A clear-sky image is the closest thing we have to a photo of the whole range at once.",
   "sections": [
    {
     "h": "Today's snow map",
     "p": [
      "Top of this list: Manning Park Resort, about 120 cm up high. Then Big White 86 cm and Marmot Basin 54 cm.",
      "Snow-covered ground in Western Canada: 201,390 km². Resorts with snow: 31 of 57.",
      "Snow-covered area: 215,390 km² on 11 November, 201,390 km² today, 210,000 km² forecast for 16 November. The map is getting less white."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Far ahead of the rest",
     "p": [
      "Manning Park Resort has 120 cm on its upper slopes, well ahead of Big White with 86 cm.",
      "At about 1,574 m, Manning Park Resort has 7 cm today; a normal 13 November has 33 cm there.",
      "Only one winter in ten has more than 77 cm on this date.",
      "A week ago the same spot had 6 cm.",
      "In past winters the best snow at Manning Park Resort usually came late March / early April.",
      "Day by day at Manning Park Resort: Saturday 14 November 2 cm; Sunday 15 November 6 cm; Monday 16 November 2 cm."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Seen from orbit",
     "p": [
      "Manning Park Resort: snow-covered in the last clear view, on 4 November. Clear sky over Big White on 4 November, and the image shows snow cover. Marmot Basin: snow-covered in the last clear view, on 4 November. Clear sky over Revelstoke on 4 November, and the image shows snow cover. Tweedsmuir Ski Club: snow-covered in the last clear view, on 10 November."
     ]
    },
    {
     "h": "Station check",
     "p": [
      "Satellites cannot see through cloud, and thin snow among trees can look patchy from orbit. Our model fills the cloudy days, and together they give a picture we trust.",
      "How sure are we? Resort values are model estimates for the upper slopes; stations are measurements, each at one point on the date shown."
     ]
    }
   ],
   "advice": [
    "South-facing slopes take the most sun and melt first; high, north-facing slopes keep their snow the longest.",
    "Snow on the map does not mean a resort is open: check lifts and opening dates on the resort's website.",
    "Early-season snow is thin. Stay on open, groomed pistes: rocks and grass can hide just under the surface.",
    "Get a powder alert in the SnowScope app to know when Manning Park Resort gets fresh snow."
   ],
   "captions": {
    "sat": "Latest clear satellite view in Western Canada (Sentinel-2 and MODIS): white = snow, light blue = patchy, brown = no snow.",
    "map": "Snow depth in Western Canada on 13 November 2026 (SnowScope snow model): Manning Park Resort, Big White, Marmot Basin, Revelstoke, Tweedsmuir Ski Club."
   }
